were removed after the runs.

The native shared-screen HUD now distinguishes connected and unavailable
reserved stations using only authenticated fixed-tick lifecycle state.
An absent remote seat shows an offline prompt, a continuing seat retains
its countdown, and an active disconnected actor and health remain visible.
Local prompts do not change; extra atlas plates are allocated only for
reserved seats. Native panel readback and the full fresh gate passed in
run 1795603871230889222-3317506.

Host idle renewal now uses the worker's monotonic decode completion time,
not a delayed caller poll. Real TLS completions with controlled observation
timestamps verify that a frame decoded before expiry can still arrive
within its renewed lease, but neither a frame decoded at expiry nor a
completion observed after its renewed lease becomes simulation input.
Both expired paths disconnect and reserve the identity for rejoin. Fresh
validation passed in run 1795604189112144995-3357907.

The three-byte wire schema is now version two, with a distinct host-only
acknowledgement that cannot decode as client input. Each valid remote
message can schedule one bounded reply through its existing fair worker
slot; replies do not renew the host's receive lease. The native controller
requires a valid acknowledgement within three seconds, and the host
rate-limits acknowledgements to at most four per second per peer.
Real TLS tests reject duplicate and oversized replies, preserve independent
peer input, sustain both leases beyond a shortened one-second limit, and
expire a controller when the still-connected host stops polling. The same
identity can then rejoin. Fresh native-enabled validation passed in run
1795604517688391544-3441994. Matching host and controller builds are now
required; the local credential catalogue remains version one. Physical
network partitions, multi-device latency and remote world display remain
unverified.

Two independent native version-two loopback suspension diagnostics used
the optimized executable, local original-derived art, separate P-256
identities, and a private X11 display with software OpenGL. With the
host process suspended but its socket open, the controller exited with
a timeout; the observed intervals were 2,981 ms and 3,004 ms (the latter
sampled at one-second intervals). After resuming the host, the same
approved identity rejoined. In the second diagnostic, comparing
960x672 captures showed exactly 1,008 changed pixels confined to the
second station's HUD label when offline; rejoining restored the
online frame pixel-for-pixel. Joining and moving that player changed
19,539 pixels in the world. All disposable private keys were removed.
This verifies native process-level liveness and visible status on one
machine, not physical network interruption or multi-device latency.

Both native title loops now cap each native event dispatch at 32 events.
The shared dispatcher preserves FIFO order, leaves the next event queued
when the budget is reached, and waits only for the initial controller
poll. Network polling, fixed updates and presentation can run between
batches. Controller replacement discovery is deferred across limited
batches until a native poll returns no event. Synthetic tests cover
queue tails, order, explicit stop and handler failure; the native render
smoke queues distinct SDL keyboard events across two-event batches.
Fresh native-enabled validation passed in run
1795605700202886000-4148650.
This bounds work by event count, not a hard wall-time deadline; physical
controller replacement and sustained real-device traffic remain open.

A four-seat native loopback diagnostic used the optimized executable from
96c56f2, verified local art, an isolated X11 display and software OpenGL.
One host and three controller processes used distinct P-256 roots and
role-specific leaves. Connecting each reserved remote changed exactly
1,008 pixels in its own HUD label; the difference rectangles were
165x15 at x=276, 516 and 756 in the 960x672 host frame. Each remote
joined and moved its corresponding actor through its controller window.
Three successive captures were identical after each release. Terminating
the third controller during held movement stabilized the host frame;
the second and fourth controllers still produced visible movement.
The same third-seat identity rejoined and moved again. All generated
private key files were removed. This covers all four seats in actual
native processes on one machine, not simultaneous physical input,
multi-device LAN or end-to-end display latency.

The host scheduler also suppresses authenticated admission completions
observed at or beyond the initial idle lease deadline. The worker closes
the unpublished connection and reserves its roster slot without sending
a phantom connect/disconnect pair to the simulation. A real TLS
regression controls the observation timestamp, queues a stale input,
requires silent cleanup, and verifies same-identity rejoin and input.

Native local input now retains one translated action batch when the
fixed-step queue is full or the event lies beyond its bounded future
window. The host stops draining further native events until that batch
can enter the queue, while transport polling and fixed updates continue.
The original timestamp is retained; the existing late-event policy
retries an already elapsed tick on the next unexecuted tick. Synthetic
tests verify that a future Start does not execute early and a rejected
movement release eventually stops movement.

A native keyboard diagnostic used the same verified art and Xvfb
software OpenGL before and after the change. Suspending the host process
for 3.4 seconds and sending Right just before resumption caused the
baseline executable (96c56f2) to exit with a future-input error.
The revised release executable survived, changed 23,400 host pixels
during movement, and produced identical captures after release.
This is a process-stall observation, not a hard input-latency bound.

The native controller atomically admits the digital edges from each
translated event. It retains one complete rejected batch while transport
polling drains the 128-edge queue, without consuming later native events.
A real-TLS regression fills 127 slots, rejects a two-edge release and
opposite press without partial admission, retries after drain, and
observes the assigned actor stop and reverse in order. Physical network
congestion remains unverified.

The clean integrated Linux release at b2b82e4 passed the four-process
native loopback diagnostic again after host and controller backpressure
changes. Each of three independently enrolled controllers joined,
moved, released and stabilized. The third disconnected while moving;
