Disposable private keys were removed; neither test establishes
physical LAN or simultaneous physical controller interoperability.

The shared native digital profile now configures signed axis action
slots and press/release hysteresis per station, including disabled
directions and positive-only triggers. The existing left-stick
defaults are preserved. Synthetic regressions exercise malformed
profiles, station isolation, inclusive thresholds, replacement
controller assignment and remote wire-edge routing. Actual
controller hardware and proportional analog movement remain open.

The native touch profile accepts a nonempty subset of local stations,
retaining the existing single-seat selection syntax and default.
The title stacks labelled panels, shares each station's remapped
button geometry between rendering and routing, and bounds contacts
by eight times the panel count. It rejects remote-reserved seats
before startup. Synthetic multi-contact tests check simultaneous
independent actions and releases across adaptive widths.
Physical touchscreen admission and device capacity remain unverified.

Multi-panel touch contacts retain the region of their initial press.
Dragging outside that region releases the current action, and returning
can resume it; dragging into another seat cannot operate its controls.
An initial press outside every panel does not acquire a seat mid-drag.
The single-panel layout retains its existing unrestricted routing.
Engine and native title regressions exercise these transitions.

Incoming native frames now retain the admitted length without allocating
its full payload on receipt of the header. Reads use a fixed-size scratch
chunk and fallible, geometrically grown storage bounded by the declared
length. The existing whole-frame deadline does not renew across chunks.
Synthetic controls admit a one-MiB header without allocation and retain
only the received prefix, then check exact multi-chunk frame boundaries.
A real mutual-TLS loopback transfers a 16,385-byte frame across polled and
blocking reads, preserves the deadline, and receives the next frame.
Fresh validation passed in run 1795608554975522202-455815. This bounds
allocation requests, not allocator-internal overhead or measured latency.

The shared frame channel now checks the existing absolute transfer
deadline again after successful or pending polled operations, and after
blocking frame completion. A late result closes the transport instead
of delivering a frame or reporting successful submission. Scripted
streams begin an operation before a partial receive's deadline, then
return successful I/O after expiry. Regressions cover both polled and
blocking receive and send, including closure of the opposite direction.
This cannot retract bytes already transmitted on the wire; it enforces
the local result and channel lifetime boundary.

The authenticated input pump preserves the monotonic worker completion
instant on each valid application message. The arcade host reports the
oldest newly admitted control timestamp and its actual fixed tick;
capacity rejection retains the original timestamp until retry succeeds.
Seat lifecycle events and heartbeats do not create control observations.
Real TLS regression tests exercise a full live input queue and verify
that the subsequently admitted timestamp is reported exactly once at
the new tick. The timestamp is host-side decode completion, not a
client clock or a network round-trip measurement.

The standalone native controller accepts an explicit `touch=on` catalogue
option. Its seven on-screen controls share the cabinet's touch geometry,
remapping, contact routing and release behavior; the host-only Pause
region is neither drawn nor mapped. Only station 1 may be touch-enabled
in its profile, regardless of the seat authenticated by the host.
Keyboard and gamepad behavior remains the default. Regression tests
exercise remapped simultaneous contacts, drag-off and focus-loss releases,
disabled touch and excluded Pause geometry. The opt-in native rendering
smoke also passes on isolated offscreen OpenGL ES. Actual touchscreen
hardware and physical multi-device LAN remain unverified.

An optimized native Xvfb loopback at 1f716d0 opened a touch-enabled
controller at 640x608 and two default controllers at 640x352. A visible
capture of the controller showed the Start glyph and background in the
excluded Pause region. All remote seats joined and moved with keyboard
input, released movement, and survived one controller's disconnect and
same-identity rejoin. The host recorded 10,891 successful submissions
and 18 remote decode-age samples before a menu-driven exit. The first
attempt could not locate the host window; the successful retry explicitly
selected SDL's X11 driver. The diagnostic did not inject touchscreen
events or use physical touch hardware.
The optimized launcher also rejected a profile selecting station 2
and duplicate touch options before opening a controller connection.

Native touch rendering and projection now run inside the existing
single-thread native controller smoke sequence to respect SDL thread
affinity even under parallel test execution. The platform smoke also
checks disabled and enabled native translation of constructed finger
events and the preserved contact identity on release.

Client acknowledgement leases are checked before receiving, at the
observation of a reply, and after bounded send polling. A reply observed
at or after expiry cannot renew the lease, even if its frame is valid.
A deterministic regression uses a real authenticated connection and
explicit observation times to exercise missing, malformed, timely and
exact-boundary acknowledgements without relying on scheduling delays.

The host input scheduler now carries its application lease through the
bounded worker queue. A receive or reply cannot start on the worker at
or after its lease expiry, even if submitted in time. A successful
operation that observes expiry on return closes the channel instead of
publishing success. This does not retract bytes already submitted to
the transport. A real TLS regression queues an acknowledgement and then
dispatches its poll with an expired lease, verifies that no frame is
delivered, and reconnects the same approved identity for valid input.

The standalone controller now uses the same persistent digital profile
selection as the cabinet. A catalogue or explicit settings directory
holds the versioned profile; explicit selection and reset override
stored and catalogue values. Publication follows authenticated connection
and successful native controller presentation setup. Regression tests
exercise precedence, reset, relative paths and rejected combinations.
Seat reservation and remote touch restrictions are unchanged.

An optimized 0716299 native loopback used a dedicated settings directory
for each endpoint, disposable mutual-TLS identities and Xvfb/software
OpenGL. The cabinet ignored its former Right key and moved with Q;
the controller ignored Right and moved with H. Both repeated this
